<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kainai Nation</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Blood tribe, Kainai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kainai, or Blood, are one of the peoples of the Blackfoot Confederacy, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siksika">Siksika</a> and Piikani. Bison hunters of the northern plains, they signed Treaty 7 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1877. Their reserve in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberta">Alberta</a>, near Cardston between the Belly and St. Mary rivers, is the largest First Nations reserve in Canada.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The people's own name, Káínaa, comes from aká, many, and nínaa, chief: Akáínaa means many chief, and Káína many chief people. The English name Blood goes back to the Plains Cree, their enemies, who called them mihkowiyiniw, stained with blood. The Kainai speak a language of the Blackfoot group, in a dialect closely related to those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siksika">Siksika</a> and Piikani. When treaties such as Treaty 7 were signed, they lived on the Oldman, Belly and St. Mary rivers west of Lethbridge, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberta">Alberta</a>.</p><p>In pre-treaty times a society called the iikunuhkahtsi was responsible for punishing misdeeds. The band government numbered 18,465 members in 2024, against 11,791 in December 2013. Its reserve, Blood 148, covers 1,410 km2 about 200 kilometres south of Calgary; ammolite mining there has uncovered mosasaur fossils, which are stored at the Royal Tyrrell Museum but belong to the nation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1960 the National Film Board of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> filmed the Kainai Sun Dance for the documentary Circle of the Sun, with the permission of tribal leaders who feared the ceremony might be dying out; its 1973 documentary Kainai dealt with the building of a factory on their land. The Kainai News, published in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberta">Alberta</a> by the Blood Indian Tribe and later by Indian News Media, was one of Canada's first aboriginal newspapers. Its first editor was Caen Bly, a granddaughter of Senator James Gladstone, and it carried editorial cartoons by Everett Soop.</p><p>The nation held the first buffalo dialogue in 2009 and in September 2014 became one of the original eight signatories of the Buffalo Treaty. In 2017 a federal court ruled that the Crown had underestimated the band's population, leaving the reserve smaller than it should have been; it could be enlarged by 421 square kilometres or the claim settled in cash. In July 2019 the nation received a $150 million settlement of a claim over Crown mismanagement of its ranching assets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The nation is governed by an elected council of twelve to fifteen members with one chief, serving four-year terms; Jim Shot Both Sides, chief from 1956 to 1980, was the last of the hereditary chiefs. In 2015 the reserve was policed by the Blood Tribe Police, with 31 officers. Among its enterprises is the mining of ammolite, a gem found only in the Bearpaw Formation and sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> for feng shui. Its communities include Standoff, Moses Lake, Fish Creek, Levern and Old Agency.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kainai Nation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early 18th century they acquired horses from the south and guns from Cree middlemen trading with the Hudson's Bay Company, and the Siksika, <b>Kainai</b> and Piikani emerged as an allied military power.</blockquote><a href="/state/blackfoot-confederacy">Blackfoot Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
